<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yodo River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yodogawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yodo River is a river of the Kansai region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, flowing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-bay">Osaka Bay</a>. For centuries it was the main water route between the capital and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. The fortified temple of Ishiyama Hongan-ji and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-castle">Osaka Castle</a> stood near its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yodo-river.jpg" alt="Yodo River" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyougushi's photos · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yodogawa_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Yodo River is the principal river of Osaka Prefecture on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, and it carries different names along its course. It leaves the southern outlet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa</a> at Ōtsu, where a dam regulates the lake's level, as the Seta River. In Kyoto Prefecture it becomes the Uji River, and after it meets the Katsura River and the Kizu River it is called the Yodo. It then flows south through Osaka into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-bay">Osaka Bay</a>; part of it runs in an artificial channel, and the old course through the city is called the Kyū-Yodo River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The basin has been a centre of Japanese politics, culture and economy since ancient times, and floods were frequent despite repeated works that began, by tradition, with the Ibaraki embankment of Emperor Nintoku's reign. Gyōki, a monk of the Nara Period, planned to cut away a hill that narrowed the Seta but gave up for fear of flooding downstream. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-kyo">Heian-kyō</a> was chosen as capital partly for the river's route to the Seto Inland Sea, and the Uji River has a prominent place in the Uji chapters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murasaki-shikibu">Murasaki Shikibu</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> rerouted the Uji and Yodo rivers when he built Fushimi Castle, completed in 1594, raising the Taikō Embankment and making Fushimi a transport hub. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a> sent sediment into the riverbeds and caused floods, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> banned the digging of tree stumps in 1660, dredged the Seta from 1670 and in 1699 carried out the large works of Kawamura Zuiken. The river remained the main route for goods between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> until the first railways of the 1870s; in 1858 about 50 boats a day carried some 1,500 people from Osaka to Fushimi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river supplies water for irrigation and powers hydroelectric generators, and more than 50 bridges cross it. The surviving remains of the Taikō Embankment in Uji were designated a National Historic Site in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yodo River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After its temple at Yamashina near Kyoto was burned by rival forces in 1532, the leadership of the Hongan-ji moved in 1533 to Ishiyama, a site on high ground at the mouth of the <b>Yodo</b> river, where a temple had been founded by Rennyo.</blockquote><a href="/state/ishiyama-hongan-ji">Ishiyama Hongan-ji</a></li></ul></section>
